(a) the sequence has an even number;
(b) the sequence has infinitely many even numbers.

positive_text:
Let $g$ be a function defined from the set $\{(a, b): a, b$ reals, $a b \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad g(a b, c)=g(a, c) g(b, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad g(a, b c)=g(a, b) g(a, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad g(a, 1-a)=1$, for all $a \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad g(a, a)=g(a,-a)=1$, for all $a \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad g(a, b) g(b, a)=1$, for all $a, b \neq 0$.

negatives.text:
