positive_text:
Dato un reticolo quadrato $2n \times 2n$, un insieme $S$ di celle è detto labirintico se soddisfa le seguenti proprietà:
- è possibile andare da ogni cella in $S$ a ogni altra cella in $S$ muovendosi solo attraverso celle adiacenti (in senso orizzontale o verticale) appartenenti a $S$;
- non esiste un ciclo in $S$, ovvero un cammino chiuso che inizi e finisca nella stessa cella di $S$ e utilizzi ogni lato al massimo una volta.

a. Mostrare che se $S$ è labirintico, allora $|S| \leq 3n^2$;

b. Costruire un insieme labirintico di 12 celle per $n=2$;

c. Definiamo perimetro di $S$ il numero di lati adiacenti a una sola cella in $S$. Determinare il perimetro di $S$ in funzione del numero $k$ di celle in $S$;

d. Provare che non esiste un insieme labirintico di 300 celle se $n=10$.

Let $a$, $b$, $c$, $p$, $q$ be positive real numbers with $p+q \geq 2$. Show that
$$
\begin{gathered}
a \sqrt{b c(a + p b)(a + q c)} + b \sqrt{a c(b + p a)(b + q c)} + c \sqrt{a b(c + p a)(c + q b)} 
\\
\leq \frac{3(p+q)}{8}(a + b)(b + c)(c + a)
\end{gathered}
$$

negatives.text:
Let $x$, $y$, $z$, $m$, $n$ be positive real numbers with $m+n \geq 2$. Show that
$$
\begin{gathered}
\sqrt{x y z (x + m y)(x + n z)} + \sqrt{x y z (y + m x)(y + n z)} + \sqrt{x y z (z + m x)(z + n y)} 
\\
\leq \frac{3(m+n)}{8}(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)
\end{gathered}
$$

Let $x$, $y$, $z$, $m$, $n$ be positive real numbers with $m+n \geq 2$. Show that
$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{x}{\sqrt{y z(x+m y)(x+n z)}} + \frac{y}{\sqrt{x z(y+m x)(y+n z)}} + \frac{z}{\sqrt{x y(z+m x)(z+n y)}} 
\\
\leq \frac{3(m+n)}{8}(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)
\end{gathered}
$$

positive_text:
A set of $n$ individuals each started with a unique item. A sequence of pairwise exchanges was carried out; each exchange involved two individuals swapping the items they currently held. Every pair of individuals exchanged at least once. After all exchanges, each person ended up with their original item. Determine the minimum number of exchanges required, for:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$.

negatives.text:
In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, two people swapped the ball they had at that moment. Each pair of people performed at least one swap. In the end each person had a different ball than the one they had at the start. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$.

In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, one person gave their ball to another person. Each pair of people performed at least one swap. In the end each person had the ball they had at the start. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$.

In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, two people swapped the ball they had at that moment. No pair of people performed more than one swap. In the end each person had the ball they had at the start. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$.

