- van iedere $a$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één rood is;
- van iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één blauw is;
- van iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één groen is;
- van iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen met $a=1$.

Een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$ noemen we goed indien we ieder geheel getal rood, blauw, groen of paars kunnen kleuren zodat
- van iedere $a$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één rood is;
- van iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één blauw is;
- van iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één groen is;
- van iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen met $a=2$, waarbij er minstens één getal moet zijn dat géén rood bevat.

positive_text:
\text{Laat } a \text{ en } b \text{ positieve reële getallen zijn.}

\text{a) Bewijs: als } a^{3} - b^{3} \geq 4a, \text{ dan geldt } a^{2} > 2b.

\text{b) Bewijs: als } a^{5} - b^{3} \geq 2a, \text{ dan geldt } a^{3} \geq 2b.

negatives.text:
\text{Laat } x \text{ en } y \text{ positieve reële getallen zijn.}

\text{a) Bewijs: als } x^{3} + y^{3} \geq 4x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{2} > 2y.

\text{b) Bewijs: als } x^{5} - y^{3} \geq 2x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{3} \geq 2y.

\text{Laat } x \text{ en } y \text{ positieve reële getallen zijn.}

\text{a) Bewijs: als } x^{3} - y^{3} \geq 4x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{2} < 2y.

\text{b) Bewijs: als } x^{5} - y^{3} \geq 2x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{3} \geq 2y.

\text{Laat } x \text{ en } y \text{ positieve reële getallen zijn.}

\text{a) Bewijs: als } x^{3} - y^{3} \geq 4x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{2} > 2y.

\text{b) Bewijs: als } x^{5} + y^{3} \geq 2x, \text{ dan geldt } x^{3} \geq 2y.

positive_text:
Bepaal alle functies $f: \mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ waarvoor geldt:
(i) voor alle $x \in \mathbb{Z}$ is $f(f(x)) = x$;
(ii) voor alle $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}$ waarvoor $x + y$ oneven is, is $f(x) + f(y) \geq x + y$.

negatives.text:
Vind alle functies $f: \mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ die voldoen aan:
(i) voor alle $x \in \mathbb{Z}$ geldt $f(f(x)) = x$;
(ii) voor alle $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}$ waarvoor $x + y$ even is, geldt $f(x) + f(y) \geq x + y$.

Vind alle functies $f: \mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ die voldoen aan:
(i) voor alle $x \in \mathbb{Z}$ geldt $f(f(x)) = x$;
(ii) voor alle $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}$ waarvoor $x + y$ oneven is, geldt $f(x) + f(y) = x + y$.

Find the number of functions $f$ from the set $S=\{0,1,2,\ldots,2020\}$ to itself such that, for all $a, b, c \in S$:
(i) If $f(a) = a$, then $a = 1$;
(ii) If $f(a) = f(b)$, then $a = b$;
(iii) If $c \equiv a + b\pmod{2021}$, then $f(c) \equiv f(a) + f(b)\pmod{2021}$.

Find the number of functions $f$ from the set $S=\{0,1,2,\ldots,2020\}$ to itself such that, for all $a, b, c \in S$:
(i) If $f(a) = a$, then $a = 0$;
(ii) If $f(a) = f(b)$, then $a = b$;
(iii) If $c \equiv ab\pmod{2021}$, then $f(c) \equiv f(a)f(b)\pmod{2021}$.

positive_text:
Given a finite non-empty collection $P$ of prime numbers, define $|P|$ as the cardinality of $P$, and $m(P)$ as the maximum length of a sequence of consecutive positive integers, each divisible by some element of $P$.
(i) Prove $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality occurring precisely when $\min(P) > |P|$;
(ii) Prove $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

negatives.text:
For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at most one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) < |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) > (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

positive_text:
Determine whether there exist a polynomial $P(y_1, y_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $C = (c_1, c_2)$ and $D = (d_1, d_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions:
(i) $C$ is an integer point (i.e., $c_1$ and $c_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|c_1 - d_1| + |c_2 - d_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $P(m_1, m_2) > P(c_1, c_2)$, for all integer points $(m_1, m_2)$ in the plane other than $C$;
(iv) $P(y_1, y_2) > P(d_1, d_2)$, for all points $(y_1, y_2)$ in the plane other than $D$.

negatives.text:
Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions:
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) < f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$, for all points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$.

Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions:
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) > f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$, for all points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$, where $f(x_1, x_2)$ is a non-negative polynomial.

Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions:
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) > f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$, for all integer points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with diagonals intersecting at right angles. A line parallel to the bases through the diagonals' intersection point intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

negatives.text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with diagonals intersecting at right angles. A line through the diagonals' intersection point is perpendicular to the bases and intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with diagonals intersecting at right angles. A line parallel to the bases through the diagonals' intersection point intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $[AD]$. Prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
1) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$ for all $m, n \in \mathbb{Z}$;
2) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for every $x \in \mathbb{Z}$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ such that:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)-2mn$ for all $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for each $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn^2$ for all $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for all $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$ for all $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) + f(1)$ is a perfect square for all $x$.

positive_text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_y^x f(t)dt - f(y)(x-y) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) < \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|^2$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) \le \frac{1}{2}|x-y|$, for every $x, y \in I$.

positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a non-increasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, with limit $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a non-increasing sequence of real numbers, with limit $0$, then there exists a non-increasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, with limit $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

negatives.text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)} + 1$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is an increasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

positive_text:
Determine all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
1) $f'(n) = 0$ for every integer $n$;
2) for any real number $x$, if $f'(x) = 0$, then $f(x) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 1$.

positive_text:
Let triangle $ABC$ be right-angled at $B$. Let $I$ be the incenter and let the incircle touch $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$, respectively. Suppose $CI$ intersects $EF$ at $M$ and $DM$ intersects $AB$ at $N$. Prove that:

a) $AI = ND$;

b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{EC}$.

negatives.text:
Let triangle $ABC$ be right-angled at $B$. Let $I$ be the incenter and let the incircle touch $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$, respectively. Suppose $CI$ intersects $EF$ at $M$ and $DM$ intersects $AB$ at $N$. Prove that:

a) $AI = DN$;

b) $FM = \frac{EI + EM}{EC}$.

Let triangle $ABC$ be right-angled at $B$. Let $I$ be the incenter and let the incircle touch $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$, respectively. Suppose $CI$ intersects $EF$ at $M$ and $DM$ intersects $AB$ at $N$. Prove that:

a) $AI = ND$;

b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{CB}$.

positive_text:
Aflați toate funcțiile diferențiabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac condițiile:
1) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
2) dacă $f'(x)=0$ pentru un $x \in \mathbb{R}$, atunci $f(x)=0$.

negatives.text:
Aflați toate funcțiile diferențiabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac condițiile:
i) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) dacă $f(x)=0$, atunci $f'(x)=0$.

Aflați toate funcțiile diferențiabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac condițiile:
i) $f'(x)=1$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) dacă $f'(x)=0$, atunci $f(x)=0$.

Aflați toate funcțiile diferențiabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac condițiile:
i) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;
ii) dacă $f'(x)=0$, atunci $f(x)=0$.

positive_text:
\text{Let } g: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \text{ be a function satisfying}
\begin{aligned}
\quad g(x+y) &\le g(x) + g(y), \\
\quad g(sx + (1-s)y) &\le s g(x) + (1-s) g(y),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R} \text{ and } s \in [0, 1].

\text{a) Show that for any } p \le q \le r \le s, \text{ where } s - r = q - p, \text{ the inequality}
\begin{aligned}
\quad g(q) + g(r) \le g(p) + g(s)
\end{aligned}
\text{is valid.}

\text{b) Demonstrate that}
\begin{aligned}
\quad g(x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n) + (n-2)(g(x_1) + g(x_2) + \dots + g(x_n)) &\ge \\
&\ge \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} g(x_i + x_j),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } n \in \mathbb{N}, \text{ } n \ge 3, \text{ and } x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}.

negatives.text:
\text{Let } f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \text{ be a function satisfying}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x+y) &\ge f(x) + f(y), \\
\quad f(tx + (1-t)y) &\le t f(x) + (1-t) f(y),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R} \text{ and } t \in [0, 1].

\text{a) Prove that for any } a \le b \le c \le d, \text{ such that } d - c = b - a, \text{ the following inequality holds}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(b) + f(c) \le f(a) + f(d).
\end{aligned}

\text{b) Prove that}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n) + (n-2)(f(x_1) + f(x_2) + \dots + f(x_n)) &\ge \\
&\ge \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} f(x_i + x_j),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } n \in \mathbb{N}, \text{ } n \ge 3, \text{ and } x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}.

\text{Let } f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \text{ be a function satisfying}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x+y) &\le f(x) + f(y), \\
\quad f(tx + (1-t)y) &\ge t f(x) + (1-t) f(y),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R} \text{ and } t \in [0, 1].

\text{a) Prove that for any } a \le b \le c \le d, \text{ such that } d - c = b - a, \text{ the following inequality holds}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(b) + f(c) \le f(a) + f(d).
\end{aligned}

\text{b) Prove that}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n) + (n-2)(f(x_1) + f(x_2) + \dots + f(x_n)) &\ge \\
&\ge \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} f(x_i + x_j),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } n \in \mathbb{N}, \text{ } n \ge 3, \text{ and } x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}.

\text{Let } f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \text{ be a function satisfying}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x+y) &\le f(x) + f(y), \\
\quad f(tx + (1-t)y) &\le t f(x) + (1-t) f(y),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R} \text{ and } t \in [0, 1].

\text{a) Prove that for any } a \le b \le c \le d, \text{ such that } d - c = b - a, \text{ the following inequality holds}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(b) + f(c) \ge f(a) + f(d).
\end{aligned}

\text{b) Prove that}
\begin{aligned}
\quad f(x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n) + (n-2)(f(x_1) + f(x_2) + \dots + f(x_n)) &\ge \\
&\ge \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} f(x_i + x_j),
\end{aligned}
\text{for all } n \in \mathbb{N}, \text{ } n \ge 3, \text{ and } x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}.

positive_text:
Let $X$ and $Y$ be positive integers, and define $\overline{XY}$ as the number formed by concatenating the digits of $Y$ to the right of the digits of $X$. For example, if $X = 193$ and $Y = 2016$, then $\overline{XY} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{XY}$ under the following conditions:

a) $X$ and $Y$ are perfect squares;

b) $X$ and $Y$ are perfect cubes;

c) $X$ is a perfect cube and $Y$ is a perfect square;

d) $X$ is a perfect square and $Y$ is a perfect cube.

Let $g: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) for all sufficiently large integers $n > N$, there exists a unique positive real number $y_n$ such that $\int_0^{y_n} g(t) \, dt = \frac{1}{n}$;
b) the sequence $(ny_n)_{n>N}$ converges and find its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) for all sufficiently large integers $n > n_0$, there exists a unique positive real number $x_n$ such that $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = -1$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

positive_text:
Let $(b_k)_{k \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic progression with positive integer terms and define $T_k = b_1^2 + b_2^2 + \dots + b_k^2$, where $k$ is a positive integer. Prove that:

a) if $q \ge 5$ is a prime, then $q$ divides $T_q$;

b) $T_5$ is not a perfect square.

negatives.text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 - a_2^2 + \dots + (-1)^{n+1} a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is a square.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the segment $CD$. Construct squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$ outwardly on the triangle $ABE$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PC$;

b) $ND$ is perpendicular to $PC$.

negatives.text:
Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ be an arbitrary point on the side $CD$. On the interior of triangle $ABE$, construct squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PC$;

b) $ND \perp PC$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ be an arbitrary point on the side $CD$. On the exterior of triangle $ABE$, construct squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PB$;

b) $ND \perp PC$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ be an arbitrary point on the side $CD$. On the exterior of triangle $ABE$, construct squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PC$;

b) $ND \parallel PC$.

positive_text:
Demonstrați că, pentru orice număr întreg pozitiv $n$ cu $n \geq 2$, există un număr $m$ nenul divizibil cu $n$ astfel încât:
i) $m < n^4$;
ii) $m$ conține în reprezentarea sa zecimală doar cel mult patru tipuri de cifre.

negatives.text:
Arătați că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăți:
a) $m > n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte.

Arătați că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăți:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc exact patru cifre distincte.

Arătați că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăți:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte, iar $m$ este pătrat perfect.

positive_text:
Let $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and define the function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$ by $f(Z) = AZ + BZ^*$, where $Z^*$ denotes the complex conjugate of the matrix $Z$. Show the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) $A + B$ and $A - B$ are invertible.

negatives.text:
Let $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and define $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$ by $f(Z) = AZ - B\bar{Z}$. Show that the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) $A + B$ and $A - B$ are non-singular.

Let $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and define $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$ by $f(Z) = AZ + B\bar{Z}^T$, where $\bar{Z}^T$ denotes the conjugate transpose of $Z$. Show the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) $A + B$ and $A - B$ are non-singular.

positive_text:
Determine all real values of $a$ and $b$ such that:
(i) $a \ge 2b^2$;
(ii) $b \ge 2a^2$;
(iii) $8(a - b)$ is an integer.

negatives.text:
Find all real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that:
(i) $x \le 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \le 2x^2$;
(iii) $8(x - y)$ is an integer.

Find all real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that:
(i) $x \ge 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) $8(x^2 - y^2)$ is an integer.

Find all real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that:
(i) $x \ge 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) $8(x + y)$ is an integer.

positive_text:
Let us define a positive integer $n$ as cool if $n$ is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $a, b, c, d$ with $a > b$ and $c > d$ such that $n = (a^3 - b^3) + (c^3 - d^3)$. For example, $225$ is cool since $225 = 15^2 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$. Prove that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) the set of cool numbers is infinite.

negatives.text:
A positive integer $n$ is called cool if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $a, b, c, d$ with $a > b$ and $c > d$ such that $n = a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. For example, $225$ is cool since $225 = 15^2 = 3^3 + 2^3 + 1^3 + 1^3$. Prove that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

A positive integer $n$ is called cool if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $a, b, c, d$ with $a > b$ and $c > d$ such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$ and $a + b + c + d$ is even. For example, $225$ is cool since $225 = 15^2 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$ and $7 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 15$, which is odd, so the example is invalid. Prove that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

positive_text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. Suppose there exist endomorphisms $\phi, \psi, \chi : G \to G$ satisfying $\phi(xy) = \psi(x)\chi(y)$ for all $x, y \in G \setminus H$. Prove that:

a) $\psi = \chi$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian and $H = Z(G)$, then $\phi = \psi = \chi$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. Suppose there exist endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G$ with $x \in H$ and $y \in G \setminus H$. Show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Determine prime numbers $p, q, r, s$, with $p \le q$ and $r \le s$, such that
(1) $p + q = r + s + 1$;
(2) $p^2 + q^2 + r^2 + s^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543^2$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a < b$ and $c < d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

positive_text:
Let $X, Y \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $X + Y = XY + YX$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(XY - YX) = 0$;
b) if $\det(X) \ne \det(Y)$, then $\det(XY - YX) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB - BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is even, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) = \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
\text{Suppose } x_{0} \text{ is a positive integer and } x_{n+1} = \sqrt{x_{n}^{2} + 1} \text{ for all } n \geq 0.

1) Show that for all } x_{0} \text{ the sequence contains infinitely many integers and infinitely many irrational numbers.

2) Does there exist an } x_{0} \text{ such that } x_{2010} \text{ is an integer?}

negatives.text:
\text{Let } a_{0} \text{ be a positive integer and } a_{n+1} = \sqrt{a_{n}^{2} - 1}, \text{ for all } n \geq 0.

1) Prove that for all } a_{0} \text{ the sequence contains infinitely many integers and infinitely many irrational numbers.

2) Is there an } a_{0} \text{ for which } a_{2010} \text{ is an integer?}

\text{Let } a_{0} \text{ be a positive integer and } a_{n+1} = \sqrt{a_{n}^{2} + 1}, \text{ for all } n \geq 0.

1) Prove that for all } a_{0} \text{ the sequence contains infinitely many integers and infinitely many irrational numbers.

2) Is there an } a_{0} \text{ for which } a_{2011} \text{ is an integer?}

positive_text:
Suppose there are $n$ languages used in KAUST. For each $k$ with $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_k$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ of the $n$ languages, and define $B_k$ as the set of individuals such that for any $k$ distinct languages among the $n$, the individual can speak at least one of them. Show that

a. If $2k \geq n+1$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
Suppose there are $n$ languages used in KAUST. For each $k$ with $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_k$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ of the $n$ languages, and define $B_k$ as the set of individuals such that for any $k$ distinct languages among the $n$, the individual can speak at least one of them. Show that

a. If $k \geq n+1$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

Suppose there are $n$ languages used in KAUST. For each $k$ with $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_k$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ of the $n$ languages, and define $B_k$ as the set of individuals such that for any $k$ distinct languages among the $n$, the individual can speak at most one of them. Show that

a. If $2k \geq n+1$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that:
1. $f(x + 1) - f(x) \geq 1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(xy) - f(x)f(y) \geq 0$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that:
1. $f(x+1) \leq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \geq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x^2) \geq f(x)^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
ext{Naj bosta } f, g: \mathbb{R} ightarrow \mathbb{R} 	ext{ funkciji z predpisoma } f(x) = -2x^2 - 4x + 6 	ext{ in } g(x) = -x + 4.

