positive_text:
Prove that there exists a constant $ c > 0 $ for which there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $ (n, N) $ satisfying:
(i) $ N > c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $, where $ k $ is the number of distinct prime factors of $ N $;
(ii) for each prime $ p $ dividing $ N $ with exponent $ e $, the integer $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) $, where $ \varphi $ is the Euler totient function, which counts the number of integers $ \leq s $ and coprime to $ s $.

negatives.text:
Prove that there is a positive real number $ c > 0 $ such that there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfying:
(a) $ N < c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $, where $ k $ is the number of distinct prime factors of $ N $;
(b) $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) $ for every prime power $ p^e $ exactly dividing $ N $.

positive_text:
Find all non-negative integer-valued functions $f$ defined on the positive integers such that:
(1) there exists some $m$ with $f(m) > 0$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^+$;
(3) there are infinitely many $n$ for which $f(k) = f(n-k)$ holds for all $k < n$.

negatives.text:
Determine all functions $f$ defined on the positive integers with non-negative integer values, such that:
(1) $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^+$;
(3) for infinitely many $n$, $f(k) = f(n+k)$ for all $k < n$.

Find all non-negative integer-valued functions $f$ defined on the positive integers satisfying:
(1) $f(n) > 0$ for at least one $n$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) \cdot f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^+$;
(3) there are infinitely many $n$ such that $f(k) = f(n-k)$ for all $k < n$.

Determine all functions $f$ defined on the positive integers with non-negative integer values satisfying:
(1) $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^+$;
(3) for infinitely many $n$, $f(k) = f(n-k)$ for all $k > n$.

positive_text:
In a network of $n$ nodes, the company establishes bidirectional connections such that the graph is connected and the out-degree of each node is the same. The company wishes to remove as few connections as possible to make the graph disconnected. What is the minimal number of connections that must be removed if:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$?

negatives.text:
There are $n$ cities in a country. A monopoly company intends to establish air traffic between some of these cities. The government demands that the air traffic satisfies the following conditions. People should be able to get from each city to any other of these cities by one or several flights. Besides there must be an equal number of flights from each city. Note that two flights — from $A$ to $B$ and from $B$ to $A$ — are considered as one flight between cities $A$ and $B$. Moreover, there may be at most one flight between two cities. But the company wants to cancel as little flights as possible so that the graph remains connected but not strongly connected. What minimal number of flights has the company to cancel if:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$?

positive_text:
Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Demonstrate that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, with:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is any non-negative integer such that $n^2$ has no zero digit in base 10;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is any non-negative integer such that $n$ has no zero digit in base 10.

negatives.text:
Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ contains only the digit 1 in the decimal record;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ contains only the digit 1 in the decimal record.

Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ does not contain zero in the decimal record. However, consider only even values of $n$.

Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ does not contain zero in the decimal record. However, define $P(n)$ as the sum of digits instead of the product.

positive_text:
There are $N$ distinct points placed on a circle. Andriy and Olesya alternate turns, starting with Andriy, drawing chords between any two of the points provided the chord does not intersect any previously drawn chord. The player who creates a triangle using three of the chords wins. Determine the winner of the game when:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$.

negatives.text:
There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya take turns, starting with Andriy, drawing chords between two points, provided the chord does not intersect any previously drawn chord. The player who completes a square (a four-sided figure) using the chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$?

There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya alternate turns, starting with Andriy, drawing chords between two points, provided the chord does not intersect any previously drawn chord. The player who cannot make a move loses. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$?

There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Andriy goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a triangle formed by the drawn chords loses. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$?

positive_text:
Determine the largest possible value of $n$ such that there exists a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$ with the following properties:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for each $i$ with $2 \le i \le n$, $a_i$ divides the product $a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

negatives.text:
What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if the following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i$ is relatively prime to $a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

positive_text:
Determine all positive integers $n$ for which an $n \times n$ square can be tiled without overlapping using $1 \times k$ rectangles and one $1 \times 1$ square, where:
a) $k=4$;
b) $k=8$.

negatives.text:
For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $2 \times 2$, where:
a) $k=4$;
b) $k=8$?

For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $1 \times 1$, where:
a) $k=3$;
b) $k=7$?

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ made of $1\times 1$ unit squares satisfying the following:
* it consists of $s$ unit squares and is not a rectangle;
* any $k\times s$ rectangle, where $k < s$, can be split into figures that coincide with $F$.

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ consisting of $1\times 1$ unit squares satisfying the following:
* it has $s$ unit squares and is not a rectangle;
* any $k\times s$ rectangle, where $k \ge s$, can be split into figures that are similar to $F$.

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ made of $1\times 1$ unit squares satisfying the following:
* it consists of $s$ unit squares and is not a rectangle;
* any $k\times s$ rectangle, where $k \ge s$, can be split into figures that coincide with $F$ or its mirror image.

positive_text:
